      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given that $(x+3)$ and $(x-1)$ are factors of $f(x)=a{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+bx-9,$ then $a+b=$",
      "worked_solution": "According to the factor theorem, if $(x-r)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ then the root $x=r$ is also a root of $f(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering the factors $(x+3)$ and $(x-1).$ The roots of these factors can be found by setting the factors equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3=0 & \\,⇒\\,x=-3 \\\\ x-1=0 & \\,⇒\\,x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, then, since $(x+3)$ and $(x-1)$ are factors of $f(x),$ the roots $x=-3$ and $x=1$ are also roots of $f(x).$ This means $f(-3)=0$ and $f(1)=0,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =0 \\\\ a(-3)^{3}+7(-3)^{2}+b(-3)-9 & =0 \\\\ -27a-3b+63-9 & =0 \\\\ -27a-3b & =-54 \\\\ -9a-b & =-18, \\\\  \\\\ f(1) & =0 \\\\ a(1)^{3}+7(1)^{2}+b(1)-9 & =0 \\\\ a+b+7-9 & =0 \\\\ a+b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives a system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-9a-b=-18 \\\\ a+b=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we eliminate $b$ and get\n\n$$\n-8a=-16\\,⇒\\,a=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=2$ in either equation gives $b=0.$ So our solutions are $a=2$ and $b=0.$ Finally then,\n\n$$\na+b=2+0=2.\n$$",
