      "name": null,
      "problem": "If $(x+5)$ is a factor of $p(x)=3{x}^{2}+14x+c,$ then $c=$",
      "worked_solution": "According to the factor theorem, if $(x-r)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ then the root $x=r$ is also a root of $p(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering the factor $(x+5).$ The root of this factor can be found by setting the factor equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+5 & =0 \\\\ x & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, then, since $(x+5)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ the root $x=-5$ is also a root of $p(x).$ This means $p(-5)=0,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(-5) & =0 \\\\ 3(-5)^{2}+14(-5)+c & =0 \\\\ 75-70+c & =0 \\\\ c & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
