      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given that $(2x-1)$ and $(x+2)$ are factors of $f(x)=a{x}^{3}+b{x}^{2}-2x$, then $a+b=$",
      "worked_solution": "According to the factor theorem, if $(x-r)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ then the root $x=r$ is also a root of $f(x).$ \n\nHere, we are considering the factors $(2x-1)$ and $(x+2).$ The roots of these factors can be found by setting the factors equal to $0$ and solving:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1=0 & \\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x+2=0 & \\,⇒\\,x=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the factor theorem, then, since $(2x-1)$ and $(x+2)$ are factors of $f(x),$ the roots $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=-2$ are also roots of $f(x).$ This means $f(\\frac{1}{2})=0$ and $f(-2)=0,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2}) & =0 \\\\ a{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}+b{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2(\\frac{1}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8}a+\\frac{1}{4}b-1 & =0 \\\\ a+2b-8 & =0 \\\\ a+2b & =8, \\\\  \\\\ f(-2) & =0 \\\\ a(-2)^{3}+b(-2)^{2}-2(-2) & =0 \\\\ -8a+4b+4 & =0 \\\\ -8a+4b & =-4 \\\\ 2a-b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives a system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}a+2b=8 \\\\ 2a-b=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEliminating $b,$ we get\n\n$$\n5a=10\\,⇒\\,a=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=2$ in either equation gives $b=3.$ So our solutions are $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Finally then,\n\n$$\na+b=2+3=5.\n$$",
